  "summary": "Floor art using colored powders, rice or flower petals to create geometric patterns at entrances of homes and temples.",
  "description": "Characterized by symmetrical mandala-like designs with mathematical precision, created as offerings to deities and to welcome prosperity. Originated in ancient India as part of daily ritual practice, with regional variations reflecting local deities and cultural motifs.",
  "culturalContext": "Rangoli — India's threshold floor art — draws daily and festival designs in rice flour, colored sand and flower petals at doorsteps and courtyards: kolam (Tamil dot-grid geometry), alpona (Bengal), mandana (Rajasthan), Onam's pookalam flower carpets, Diwali's diyas-ringed designs. The practice (women's daily ritual) binds welcome, auspiciousness and bird-feeding (the flour feeds ants); rangoli competitions and tattoo adaptations carry the geometric folk grammar worldwide.",
